      "content": "Yes — 'when tips don't come in' is the context clue, and surviving on basic wage only is exactly the idea. 'Rainy days' means difficult times, especially when money is tight.\n\n**Model Answer for Exam:**\n> 'Rainy days' means times of financial hardship, difficulty, or unforeseen trouble/emergencies — days of struggle, need or challenge when tips run dry. (1)\n\n---\n### 📖 Concept & Thought Process (How to Understand It):\n* The Concept / Rule: An idiomatic expression has a figurative meaning, not a literal one. To answer it, you must use the surrounding context, not the dictionary meaning of the individual words.\n* The Thought Process (How to Unpack It):\n  1. Step 1 — Spot the idiom: 'rainy days' cannot be about weather here because the passage is about wages and tips.\n  2. Step 2 — Find
